# NOTE: EAS archives from the GIT ROOT, which is the parent of this directory,
# so the file it actually consults is ../.easignore. This one is kept correct
# and unanchored in case the project root is ever used, but the root file is
# the one that governs. See its header for the 1.8 GB incident.

# What NOT to upload to EAS Build. EAS runs its own install and prebuild on the
# server, so the heavy local folders below are dead weight in the upload archive
# (the first preview build shipped 214 MB without this file). Everything the
# build actually needs — app/, src/, assets/, app.json, eas.json, package*.json,
# and the config files — is deliberately NOT listed here.

# Dependencies (EAS reinstalls from the lockfile)
node_modules/

# Git history (not needed to build)
.git/

# Native folders (EAS prebuilds these from app.json on the server)
#
# UNANCHORED ON PURPOSE, and this is the bug that made the root .easignore
# necessary. These were `/android` and `/ios`. A leading slash anchors a pattern
# to the ARCHIVE root, and EAS archives from the GIT root — which is the parent
# directory, not this one. So they meant `Ealchapp/android`, which does not
# exist, and `ealch-v2/android` uploaded whole: 1,566 MB of a 1.8 GB archive on
# 2026-08-20. The unanchored entries in this file kept working the whole time,
# which is exactly why it looked fine.
android/
ios/

# Expo / build caches and outputs
.expo/
dist/
web-build/
*.tsbuildinfo

# Local noise
*.log
.DS_Store

# Markdown docs never belong in a build upload (README/AGENTS/CLAUDE/etc.)
*.md
